      "No board, no fins, no intermediary between you and the wave — bodysurfing is the oldest form of wave riding, and in many ways the most honest.",
    body: `## Before the Board

Long before Polynesians shaped planks of koa wood and rode them standing upright, people were riding waves with their bodies. The practice predates surfing by an unknowable span of time; it requires nothing but a wave and the willingness to meet it. And yet, in the modern water sports landscape dominated by surfboards, stand-up paddleboards, bodyboards, and an expanding zoo of foiling craft, bodysurfing occupies a curiously marginal position. It is sometimes dismissed as what you do when you have forgotten your board. The dismissal is entirely wrong.

Bodysurfing demands an intimacy with the wave that equipment-dependent sports cannot match. When you drop into a wave bodysurfing, you are committed completely — there is no board to lean on, no buoyancy other than your own, no mechanism to redirect your trajectory other than your body position and the hydroplaning lift of your leading hand. The feedback is immediate and unforgiving. You either understand the wave or you do not, and if you do not, you get held under until it decides to let you go.

The reward for that intimacy is a sensation that surfers sometimes describe jealously: you feel the wave directly, from inside, the way you feel the road surface through thin running shoes rather than through thick cushioned soles.

## The Hand Plane

While true purists ride waves with their arms alone, most bodysurfers use a hand plane — a small buoyant surface, roughly the size of a hardback book, worn on one hand. Its function is to create lift at the front of the body, raising the chest and head clear of the water and allowing the bodysurfer to plane across the face of the wave rather than simply being pushed forward by the whitewater.

Hand planes are made from timber, foam, or fibreglass. Wooden versions — particularly those shaped from paulownia or marine ply — have an almost sculptural quality and a following among enthusiasts who appreciate the craft. Fibreglass planes shaped by small independent makers can be precisely tuned for different wave sizes and riding styles. Foam versions are cheaper and perfectly functional for beginners.

The technique for using a hand plane involves extending the planing hand forward and slightly downward as you catch the wave, keeping the elbow just slightly bent so the plane can flex and trim. The free arm tucks against the body or is used for steering. Getting this right takes time — a few sessions, typically — but when it clicks, the difference in speed and trim is dramatic.

## Catching Waves

The physical process of catching a wave bodysurfing requires more fitness and timing than board-based riding. Without a board's paddle momentum, you must generate your own. The technique involves positioning yourself at the right depth — typically chest to shoulder height — and reading the approaching wave carefully enough to begin your sprint before it arrives.

The sprint itself is hard work. Two or three powerful freestyle strokes, then a single dolphin kick as the wave picks you up, then one hand extends forward with the plane while you arch your lower back slightly to angle your body down the face. Get it right and the wave takes you; miss the timing and you come up behind the broken lip watching the wave continue without you.

Bodysurfers use fins — short, stiff freediving fins rather than the longer, flexible fins used in snorkelling — to generate the burst of speed needed to match the wave. Without fins, catching anything other than a very steep, pitching shore break is extremely difficult. Churchill and DaFiN are the two fins most widely used in the bodysurfing community; both provide excellent thrust with minimal drag.

## Finding the Right Wave

Bodysurfing is most rewarding in certain conditions that do not necessarily coincide with what a surfer would choose. Steep, punchy, hollow shore break — waves that pitch and barrel close to the beach — is ideal. These waves are often considered too unpredictable or dangerous for surfboards, but a bodysurfer who can read and time them well is perfectly placed.

In the UK, beaches with steeper profiles and shore dumping waves offer the best bodysurfing. The Gower in South Wales, parts of the North Cornwall coast around Newquay, and exposed west-facing beaches in Pembrokeshire all have appropriate conditions. Bodysurfing in two to four foot shore break on a warm summer day, with good visibility and sand underfoot, is as simple and satisfying as the ocean gets.

Avoid bodysurfing directly in front of surf schools or in crowded board areas — without the visual presence of a board, bodysurfers can be difficult for other water users to spot.

## Technique Inside the Wave

The real artistry of bodysurfing reveals itself once you are inside the wave. The leading hand and plane control trim — press down and you angle deeper, raise it and you ride higher on the face. The hips steer. A slight twist of the torso redirects the line, sending you tracking along the shoulder of the wave instead of straight to the beach.

Experienced bodysurfers pull into barrels. Standing inside a pitching hollow wave with no board between you and the spinning concave interior is an experience that even veteran surfers describe with something approaching reverence. The tube ride lasts perhaps a second, occasionally more, and the senses overload completely: the noise, the compression of air, the green-white light at the exit, and then the explosion of whitewater as it closes. There is nothing quite like it in any sport.

## The Tradition

Bodysurfing has a rich lineage in surf culture that is easy to overlook. Mark Cunningham, a Honolulu lifeguard and bodysurfer of extraordinary skill, spent decades riding the steep shore break at Pipeline on the North Shore of Oahu without a board, earning respect from the world's best surfers for his ability to ride waves that injured people on boards. The bodysurfing community is small but serious, and its self-conception as the discipline closest to the original spirit of wave riding carries real weight.

Come to bodysurfing with patience and it will pay you back with waves that feel like they belong entirely to you.`,
