    title: "Atlantic vs Mediterranean: Two Kinds of Sea",
    img: "turquoise",
    excerpt:
      "Same planet, same water — and yet the Atlantic and Mediterranean are almost entirely different oceans to swim in, surf in, and be shaped by.",
    body: `## A Tale of Two Waters

Stand on a beach in Cornwall and stand on a beach in Mallorca, and the sea in front of you looks different in ways that are not simply about the colour of the sky or the presence of umbrellas. The Atlantic is grey-green and restless, subject to a swell that arrives from thousands of miles away, pushed by storms you will never see. The Mediterranean is blue-clear and dense with salt, largely contained, its surface behaviour governed by local winds rather than oceanic groundswell. Both are magnificent. They are also almost entirely unlike each other, and understanding the differences matters if you swim, surf, dive, or simply spend time near either one.

This is not an argument for one over the other. The Atlantic has a power and a changeability that can be awe-inspiring or dangerous or both simultaneously. The Mediterranean has a warmth and clarity that the Atlantic can only achieve in tropical latitudes. They serve different moods, different activities, different relationships with the sea.

## Temperature and Salinity

The Mediterranean is considerably saltier than the Atlantic. Enclosed by land on all sides except the narrow Strait of Gibraltar, it loses more water to evaporation than it gains from rivers and rainfall. The resulting salinity — around 38 to 39 parts per thousand, compared to roughly 35 for the open Atlantic — gives Mediterranean water a noticeably higher buoyancy. Swimmers frequently comment on how much easier it is to float in the Med; this is not imagination, it is physics.

Temperature differences are equally striking. Mediterranean surface water in summer ranges from around 25 degrees Celsius in the western basin to 28 or 29 degrees in the eastern Aegean and Levantine Sea. The Atlantic coast of the UK sits at the opposite end of the spectrum: 14 to 17 degrees on a good summer day in Cornwall, 10 degrees or below in Scotland in winter. Even the warmest Atlantic waters at the Azores and Canaries rarely exceed 23 degrees. For swimmers, this is the defining experiential difference between the two bodies of water.

## Waves and Swell

The Atlantic is a wave-generating machine. The vast fetch of open water — thousands of miles in which storms can build and organize swell — means that groundswell arrives on west-facing Atlantic coastlines with regularity and power. A swell generated by a storm off Newfoundland can travel for five or six days and arrive on the coast of Cornwall or Galicia with wave faces of six, eight, or ten feet. The periodicity (the time between wave crests) is long — twelve to eighteen seconds on a well-organized groundswell — which produces waves that feel powerful and weighty, with real energy behind them.

The Mediterranean is largely a wind-wave sea. Enclosed and relatively shallow in places, it does not have the fetch to generate true oceanic groundswell, and wave periods rarely exceed eight seconds. This means surf, where it exists, tends to be choppy, confused, and less powerful than Atlantic equivalents. There are exceptions — the Gulf of Lion off southern France and the Tramontane-affected waters of Catalonia and the Costa Brava can produce surprisingly respectable surf in autumn and winter — but by Atlantic standards the Mediterranean is a flat-water environment.

## Tides

One of the most practically significant differences between the two seas is tidal range. The Atlantic is a properly tidal ocean: spring tides on the Cornish coast produce ranges of five to six metres, and the Bristol Channel reaches the second-highest tidal range in the world at around fourteen metres at Avonmouth. This massive rise and fall transforms beaches, exposes reefs, drains harbours, and makes tidal knowledge essential for any coastal activity.

The Mediterranean is very nearly tideless. Tidal range across most of the Mediterranean is less than half a metre, and in enclosed areas like the Adriatic it can be measured in centimetres. For sailors and kayakers accustomed to working with tidal streams, this takes adjustment. For beach visitors, it means the shoreline stays roughly where you left it, and the rockpool exposed at breakfast is still accessible at sunset.

## Marine Life

The Atlantic and Mediterranean host different but overlapping marine communities. Atlantic waters off the UK coast produce excellent diving for those willing to tolerate cold and reduced visibility: kelp forests, seal colonies, diverse reef fish, and in the right seasons, dolphins, basking sharks, and the occasional humpback whale. The cold water suppresses visibility compared to tropical seas but the biodiversity and sheer biomass of Atlantic reef systems is remarkable.

Mediterranean visibility, in clean inshore waters, can reach thirty metres or more — exceptional for a European sea. The warm, clear water makes snorkelling a genuine pleasure even without specialist equipment, and Mediterranean rocky shores host beautiful communities of sea urchins, octopus, wrasse, and in deeper water, the brilliant orange of cup corals and gorgonian sea fans. However, decades of overfishing have significantly depleted fish populations in many areas, and the contrast with North Atlantic reef systems — where legal protection has allowed stocks to rebuild — is sometimes stark.

## The Psychological Difference

Beyond the measurable differences in temperature, wave height, salinity, and tidal range, there is a quality to each sea that is less easily quantified. The Atlantic commands a different kind of attention. Its changeability — the way a flat-calm morning can become a white-capped afternoon, the way the colour shifts from jade to slate as cloud crosses the sun — keeps you alert, keeps you reading it. The sea feels alive in a way that can tip toward the uncomfortable.

The Mediterranean, at its summer best, offers something the Atlantic rarely provides in the UK: the possibility of genuine ease. Floating in clear warm water with no wetsuit, looking down at a rocky bottom ten metres below, on a still August afternoon — that is a relationship with the sea that is wholly different in character, and no less valuable for it. The best coastal life accommodates both.`,
