    title: "Packing for a Surf Trip: The Only List You Need",
    img: "turquoise",
    excerpt:
      "Strip away the gear-anxiety and packing paranoia, and a surf trip kit list is simpler than you think — here is what actually matters, and why.",
    body: `## The Overpacking Trap

The standard surf trip packing experience involves laying out your gear the night before departure, looking at the pile, concluding it is probably not enough, adding things, concluding you now have too much, removing things, and eventually boarding the vehicle with approximately the same amount you started with. This is universal. It does not get better with experience — it merely gets faster.

The solution is not a better system. It is a clearer understanding of what you will actually use, and a willingness to commit to that list rather than hedging against every conceivable scenario. Most surf trips involve fewer surprises than the imagination suggests. The sea is cold or it is not. The waves are good or they are not. The roof rack holds or it does not. Planning for the realistic rather than the catastrophic saves weight, saves space, and saves the low-grade anxiety of managing equipment you never needed.

What follows is a functional list for a UK or European surf trip of three to seven days, travelling by car or van. It is not exhaustive — adjust for your skill level, destination, and travel style — but it covers everything that actually matters.

## The Surfboard Question

Start here because it is the most important decision and the one with the most obvious physical constraint. If you are driving, you can take two boards comfortably on a roof rack and a third in the car if necessary. Take a board appropriate for the expected conditions and a backup in a slightly different volume or shape for when conditions surprise you. A shortboard and a mid-length or mini-mal covers most realistic UK scenarios well.

If you are flying, the economics of board bags on budget airlines have become painful in recent years. Many experienced travellers flying short-haul to Portugal, France, or the Canaries now hire boards at the destination rather than pay the bag fee, check carefully, and survive the anxiety of the baggage carousel. If you are travelling to somewhere where hire quality is unpredictable, pay the fee and bring your own.

A quality board bag with at least six millimetres of foam padding is non-negotiable if you are flying or travelling long distances by car. The road vibration on an unpadded board over several hundred miles produces dings; dings produce water ingress; water ingress ruins boards.

## Wetsuits and Neoprene

For UK surfing, your wetsuit is your most important piece of kit and the one you should never compromise on. In summer (June to September) a 3/2mm suit is the working standard for most surfers in Cornwall and Devon; in spring and autumn this becomes a 4/3mm, and winter demands a 5/4mm or 5mm suit with hood, gloves, and boots. Pack one size warmer than you think you need — conditions vary, and being able to surf comfortably in unexpectedly cold water is worth the boot bag space.

Bring one wetsuit for every three days of a trip; rinse and dry between sessions, and it will keep. A wetsuit hanger — a wide, shaped hanger that allows air to circulate through the suit rather than fold it at the shoulders — is worth packing if you have room.

## Surf Accessories

This is the category most prone to over-packing. What you actually need:

- Wax (appropriate temperature rating for your destination: cold water wax for the UK; tropical wax for the Canaries or further afield)
- A wax comb for re-texturing
- A deck grip pad if you use one, already fitted to the board
- Fin key and a full set of spare fins for your fin system (a fin lost in a wipeout is a session-ending event; a spare set is four minutes' packing)
- Ding repair resin and tape — not for mid-trip repairs necessarily, but to seal a ding temporarily until proper repair is possible
- Leash — check it for wear at the cuff and at the rail saver before departure; a snapped leash in heavy surf is dangerous

## Clothing and Layers

Pack for the gap between sessions as much as for the sessions themselves. The cold after a surf — standing in a car park or at a beach cafe, still damp despite your best towelling efforts — is where most people discover they have packed wrong. Warm layers that pack small and dry quickly are the priority.

A fleece or mid-layer, a waterproof outer shell, and one set of warm evening clothing covers most European destinations in shoulder seasons. Add a second base layer if you run cold. Reduce all of this significantly for a summer trip to the Canaries or Morocco. A changing robe or surf poncho for car-park changing is worth every millimetre of boot space it occupies.

## First Aid and Essentials

A small kit tailored to surf injuries: antiseptic for reef cuts (which get infected quickly in warm tropical water), zinc-oxide tape for fin cuts, paracetamol and ibuprofen, and eye drops for salt water irritation. Reef booties for anywhere with known urchin or sharp coral exposure.

Sun protection deserves a serious section. Surfers burn badly and frequently — water reflects UV, you are horizontal and face-up paddling for hours, and cloud does not reliably protect. Use a high-factor waterproof SPF on the face and back of the neck for every session, regardless of whether the sky looks sunny.

## What to Leave Behind

Board shorts in six colours. The third wetsuit you might use if the first two are both wet simultaneously. The waterproof camera you brought on the last two trips and left in the van both times. The book you have been meaning to read for four months. Half the wax you brought because you might run out.

Surf trips have a way of becoming very simple, very quickly. The wave quality dominates every conversation and every decision. You wake up when it is light, you check the surf, you surf, you eat, you surf again if it is good, you eat more, you sleep. In that rhythm, the excess in your bag starts to feel irrelevant within forty-eight hours. Pack light enough to get there easily, heavy enough to stay warm and functional. Everything else follows.`,
