      "Tides govern everything at the coast — where you can surf, when you can launch, whether the beach exists — and understanding them properly transforms your time by the sea.",
    body: `## The Pull of the Moon

Tides are among the most reliable and predictable natural phenomena on earth, and yet they remain poorly understood by most people who spend time near the sea. The basic mechanism is gravitational: the moon exerts a tidal force on the earth, pulling the ocean toward it on the side nearest the moon and creating a corresponding bulge on the far side through inertia. As the earth rotates beneath this double-bulge pattern, coastal locations experience the regular rise and fall of the tide — typically twice per day, at intervals of roughly twelve hours twenty-five minutes.

The sun also exerts a tidal force — smaller than the moon's but significant — and it is the interaction between the two that produces the spring and neap cycle. When the sun, moon, and earth are aligned (at new moon and full moon), the solar and lunar forces combine to produce the highest high tides and lowest low tides of the month: spring tides. When the sun and moon are at right angles to each other (at the quarter moon phases), they partially cancel each other out, producing neap tides with a smaller range between high and low water.

This cycle repeats every two weeks, and understanding it is fundamental to coastal planning. Spring tides drain harbours completely, expose reefs that are never seen at neap, and make tidal rip currents significantly more powerful. The week around spring tides is when the coast is at its most dramatic and demands the most respect.

## Tidal Range in the UK

The UK is exceptional, globally speaking, for tidal range. The Bristol Channel has the second-highest tidal range in the world — up to fourteen metres at Avonmouth during spring tides — which means the difference between high and low water is a fourteen-metre vertical shift in sea level. Even on the comparatively modest tidal coasts of East Anglia or the Solent, ranges of three to five metres during springs produce substantial horizontal shifts across shallow beaches.

This has profound practical implications. A beach that is a hundred metres wide at low tide may be a narrow strip of sand at high tide — or entirely underwater. A shallow rocky reef that produces excellent surf at low to mid tide may be too deep to break at high water. A harbour entrance that is navigable at mid tide may be impassable at low water. For everyone who uses the coast — surfers, sea kayakers, swimmers, sailors, coast-walkers — tidal awareness is not optional knowledge. It is safety-critical.

## Reading Tide Tables

Tide tables are published for standard ports — official reference points distributed around the coast — and the tables give the time and height of each high and low water. For UK waters, the Admiralty Tide Tables are the authoritative source; many digital services (Tides.net, the Met Office app, Windy.com) provide the same information in accessible formats.

The height given in tide tables is metres above Chart Datum — the lowest predicted tide level at that location. A high water height of 5.2 metres means the sea surface is 5.2 metres above Chart Datum at that moment. A low water height of 0.4 metres means it is 0.4 metres above the lowest it ever theoretically gets.

Secondary port corrections allow you to calculate tide times and heights for locations other than the standard port. If your tide table covers Plymouth but you are surfing at Newquay, a simple correction factor (available in almanacs and online) adjusts the time and height to your location. This correction can be significant — up to an hour or more in some areas.

## The Rule of Twelfths

Water does not rise and fall at a steady rate throughout the tide. It accelerates through the middle hours of the flood or ebb and moves more slowly near high and low water — a pattern described by the Rule of Twelfths. The rule states that in the first hour after low water, the tide rises by one-twelfth of its total range; in the second hour, two-twelfths; in the third hour, three-twelfths; in the fourth hour, three-twelfths; in the fifth hour, two-twelfths; in the sixth hour, one-twelfth.

The practical implication: the tide is moving fastest — and tidal streams are strongest — in the middle hours of the cycle. This is when rip currents are most powerful, when tidal streams in headland channels run fastest, and when the sea level changes most rapidly. Exposed rock traverses and coastal walks that need to be completed before the tide reaches a certain height need to account for this acceleration.

## How Tides Affect Surfing

For surfers, tide timing is one of the primary variables in wave quality. Most surf breaks have a preferred tidal state — low, mid, or high — that determines when they produce their best waves. The relationship is specific to the shape of the seabed.

Reef breaks typically work best at a specific tide height that provides enough water to prevent the wave from catching on shallow rock (too low and the wave closes out over the reef; too high and the depth removes the energy-focusing effect that creates the quality wave). The Thurso East reef in Scotland, for example, is widely considered to produce its best waves at low to mid tide; too much water and the wave loses its characteristic pitch and hollow character.

Beach breaks are generally more forgiving but still have tidal preferences. Steep beaches with shore-dumping waves often produce better shape at higher tide; gently sloping beaches may offer longer rides at lower tide when the waves have more distance to travel.

Checking a break's tide preferences before driving hours to surf it is basic research. Local surfers, surf shop staff, and surf report services all carry this knowledge. Ask, and factor it in.

## Tidal Streams and Rips

Tidal streams — horizontal water movement driven by the tide — are distinct from but related to rip currents, which are driven by wave setup. On a coastline with significant tidal range, tidal streams flow parallel to the coast (and through headland channels at high speed), while rip currents flow offshore perpendicular to the beach in localised channels.

Both demand respect. Tidal streams off headlands, during spring tides at mid-flood or mid-ebb, can exceed three knots — fast enough to prevent a strong swimmer from making progress against them. Rip currents in surf are discussed elsewhere (in the context of beach safety), but tidal awareness provides the background context: a rip current running in the same direction as the ebb stream is significantly stronger than the same physical rip running in slack water.

Tide awareness is the foundation of coastal safety and coastal opportunity. Learn it, use it, and the sea opens up in ways that are only available to those who understand its rhythm.`,
